On Thu, 3 Aug 2006, John H. wrote:
> Arthur J. O'Dwyer wrote:
>>    However, I'm neutral on the safes. They seem not out of character,
>> and they might provide some interesting interactions.
>>
>>    Wands of opening should never work on safes; and "knock" only
>> when cast at very high skill levels. "The safe door jiggles."
>
> The knock thing perhaps, but wands of opening are not actually that
> common, and could stand being a bit more useful.

   That's precisely why I /don't/ think they should work. Opening a
safe should be /difficult/, and take /resources/. Everybody has plenty
of unused and otherwise-useless wands of opening lying around; if
you made them open safes, well, you might as well make safes openable
with mirrors or tin whistles. :)

   (Clarification based on previous reply: Yeah, that would break
realism, but I'm just talking about the in-game effects. You're taking
a currently useless item and turning it into a way to get gold easily.
I consider that a bad thing, because there's no tradeoff involved;
it's just giving a gift to the player.)

>> Skeleton keys should work only rarely on safes; after all, they're
>> designed to prevent thieves from breaking in, so obviously they'd
>> resist skeleton keys!
>
> According to the idea, skeleton keys would never work on safes, a
> different (probably rarer) kind of key would be used for them

   (Yes, but I'm opposed to specialized key types.)


> Another way could be to apply a stethoscope with a high dexterity.

   Of course! ...Well, technically you'd have to have a lockpick also,
unless this is a combination safe, in which case the idea of a "safe
key" is right out. (Some combination locks have backup "master key"
opening mechanisms, but I don't think a safe would.)
   Making it a combination safe would conveniently explain why keys
don't work in it. But you'd have to code up some way to try different
combinations, possibly making it a mini-game like the castle tune,
and that would take much more coding, I think.

